  "On 1 July 1916, the first day of the Battle of the Somme, the British army suffered 57,470 casualties, 19,240 of them killed; the battle went on until 18 November. [[c-ww1-somme]] Britannica estimates that some 8.5 million soldiers died of wounds or disease, and that more civilians died, around 13 million, mainly of hunger, harsh conditions and illness or in fighting and mass killings. [[c-ww1-deaths]] Then came influenza. In 1918-19 an H1N1 virus infected about a third of the world's population and killed at least 50 million people, about 675,000 of them in the United States, and its high death rate among healthy people, including those aged 20 to 40, was unique to this pandemic. [[c-ww1-flu]]",
