  "Mustafa Kemal had made Ankara his headquarters in 1919, during the nationalist resistance after the war, and in 1923 it was declared Turkey's capital. [[c-peace-ankara]] At Lausanne on 24 July 1923, Turkey and seven states, among them Britain, France and Greece, signed the final treaty of the First World War, which recognized the boundaries of modern Turkey. [[c-peace-lausanne]] Six months earlier a convention had provided for the exchange of the Greek Orthodox and Muslim populations of Turkey and Greece, except the Greeks of Istanbul and the Muslims of Western Thrace: about 1.2 million Greeks left Turkey and about 400,000 Muslims left Greece. [[c-peace-exchange]] On 29 October 1923 the assembly declared Turkey a republic, with Mustafa Kemal as its first president. The caliphate was abolished in March 1924, and in 1928 Islam ceased to be the state religion. [[c-peace-republic]]",
