  "The Great Depression began in August 1929 and hit bottom in March 1933, when the commercial banking system collapsed. [[c-dep-start]] The stock market had peaked on 3 September 1929; on 28 and 29 October the Dow Jones index fell by nearly 13 and nearly 12 percent, and by July 1932 it stood 89 percent below its peak. [[c-dep-crash]] Yet the crash's impact 'faded within a few months'. Problems elsewhere in the financial system turned a short recession into a depression, and some economists, such as Meltzer, see the crash as a symptom rather than a cause. [[c-dep-crash-role]] From 1930 to 1933 about 9,000 American banks suspended operations, [[c-dep-banks]] and the money supply fell by nearly 30 percent. [[c-dep-money]] Unemployment in 1933 was 24.9 or 20.6 percent, depending on whether people on work relief are counted as unemployed (15.0). [[c-dep-unemp-defs]]",
